export const DATA_53: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-067", num: 67, title: "Đề 067", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Rise of Cooking Vlogs", paragraphs: ["Over the past ten years, cooking videos on the internet have become one of the most popular types of online content. Millions of people around the world now watch videos of home cooks and professional chefs preparing meals, and many of these viewers try the recipes themselves at home. This kind of video content is commonly known as a cooking vlog — a combination of the words \"video\" and \"blog\" — and the people who create them are called vloggers. Today, cooking vlogs can be found on platforms such as YouTube, Instagram, and TikTok, and some of the most successful creators have millions of followers from countries all over the world.", "One reason why cooking vlogs have become so popular is that they are easy to watch and understand. Unlike written recipes, which can sometimes be difficult to follow, videos allow viewers to see exactly how each step is done. A viewer can watch how to chop an onion, how to check if oil is hot enough for frying, or how to tell when a cake is properly baked — all things that are very difficult to describe clearly in words alone. For people who are learning to cook for the first time, watching a video is often much more helpful than reading instructions from a book.", "Another reason for the growing popularity of cooking vlogs is that they often feel personal and friendly. Many vloggers talk directly to the camera while they cook, sharing stories about their families, their childhood memories of food, or the reasons why a particular dish is special to them. This style of presentation makes viewers feel as though they are cooking alongside a friend rather than following instructions from a stranger. Some vloggers also respond to comments left by their audience, answer questions about ingredients, and suggest variations for people with different dietary needs, which helps build a strong sense of community around their channel.", "Cooking vlogs cover an enormous variety of topics and styles. Some creators focus on quick and easy meals that can be prepared in under thirty minutes, which are especially popular among busy working adults and students. Others specialize in dishes from specific countries or cultures, helping viewers discover the flavors and cooking techniques of cuisines they may never have tried before. There are also vlogs dedicated to baking, vegetarian and vegan cooking, street food, traditional family recipes, and even the science behind why certain cooking methods produce better results than others. Whatever a viewer is interested in, there is almost certainly a cooking vlog that matches their tastes.", "The success of cooking vlogs has also had a significant impact on the food industry. Restaurants and food companies have noticed that dishes featured in popular vlogs often see a sharp rise in demand almost immediately. When a vlogger shares a recipe using a particular ingredient or product, sales of that item can increase dramatically within days. Some food brands now pay well-known vloggers to feature their products in videos, a practice known as sponsored content. While most vloggers are open about these arrangements with their audience, some critics argue that sponsored content can make it harder for viewers to know whether a recommendation is genuine or simply paid for.", "Not all feedback about cooking vlogs is positive. Some health professionals have expressed concern that many popular cooking videos promote meals that are high in fat, sugar, and salt, and that regularly watching and recreating such recipes could encourage unhealthy eating habits. Others point out that the equipment and ingredients shown in some vlogs can be expensive, which may make certain viewers feel that good cooking is only possible with costly tools or hard-to-find products. However, many vloggers have responded to this kind of criticism by creating content that focuses specifically on healthy, affordable cooking using simple, everyday ingredients.", "Despite these concerns, cooking vlogs remain one of the most watched categories of online video content worldwide. For many people, they have made cooking feel less like a difficult chore and more like an enjoyable and creative activity. Whether someone wants to learn a new skill, try a dish from another country, or simply find inspiration for what to cook for dinner, a cooking vlog is never more than a few clicks away."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Cooking vlogs have become widely popular due to their accessible and personal style, and while they bring many benefits, they also raise some concerns.", "The most successful cooking vloggers earn large amounts of money through sponsored content and brand partnerships.", "Cooking vlogs are replacing traditional cookbooks and professional cooking schools around the world.", "Cooking vlogs are harmful because they encourage people to eat unhealthy food and spend too much money on ingredients."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are cooking vlogs considered more helpful than written recipes for beginners?", options: ["Because videos let viewers see each cooking step being performed, which is difficult to convey clearly in writing.", "Because videos are shorter and easier to find online than printed recipe books.", "Because written recipes often contain mistakes that video creators have already corrected.", "Because cooking vlogs always include a full list of ingredients with exact prices and measurements."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do many cooking vloggers create a sense of community with their audience?", options: ["By organizing live cooking events where viewers can attend and cook alongside the vlogger in person.", "By collaborating with professional chefs to ensure that all recipes shown are nutritionally balanced.", "By publishing written versions of their recipes in printed magazines for viewers who prefer not to watch videos.", "By sharing personal stories, responding to comments, and offering advice for viewers with different dietary needs."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"chop\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["mix together", "boil", "cut into pieces", "peel"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is \"sponsored content\" in the context of cooking vlogs?", options: ["Videos in which vloggers are paid by food brands to feature their products.", "Videos that are funded by the government to promote healthy eating habits.", "Cooking videos that are only available to viewers who pay a monthly subscription fee.", "Content created by professional chefs who receive free cooking equipment from manufacturers."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is a concern raised by health professionals about cooking vlogs?", options: ["That many popular cooking videos feature recipes that are high in fat, sugar, and salt.", "That cooking vlogs discourage young people from learning to cook by making the process look too simple.", "That the equipment shown in cooking videos is often dangerous for inexperienced home cooks to use.", "That vloggers often give incorrect food safety advice, which could cause viewers to become ill."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the personal stories shared by vloggers during their videos.", "the food brands that pay for sponsored content.", "cooking vlogs.", "the viewers who watch videos and leave comments."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"dramatically\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["large and striking", "temporary and short-lived", "slow and barely noticeable", "unexpected and confusing"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following types of cooking vlog is NOT mentioned?", options: ["Vlogs focused on vegetarian and vegan cooking.", "Vlogs dedicated to the science behind cooking methods.", "Vlogs that teach viewers how to grow their own vegetables at home.", "Vlogs specializing in dishes from specific countries or cultures."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 5 and 6, what can be inferred about cooking vloggers' response to criticism?", options: ["Some vloggers have adapted their content in response to concerns, for example by creating healthier and more affordable recipes.", "Vloggers have stopped accepting sponsored deals entirely after critics raised concerns about honesty.", "Most vloggers ignore criticism and continue to produce the same type of content regardless of audience feedback.", "Vloggers rely on food companies to decide what kind of content is appropriate to publish on their channels."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Landslides in India: Causes and Consequences", paragraphs: ["India is one of the countries most frequently affected by landslides, with the Western Ghats, the Himalayas, and the northeastern hill states accounting for the majority of incidents recorded each year. A landslide occurs when a large mass of rock, soil, or debris moves rapidly down a slope under the force of gravity, often triggered by an event that destabilizes the ground. In India, landslides claim hundreds of lives annually and cause extensive damage to roads, bridges, buildings, and agricultural land. Despite their recurrence, landslides remain one of the least understood natural hazards in the country, and efforts to predict and prevent them have met with only partial success.", "The causes of landslides in India can be divided into natural and human-induced factors, though the two categories frequently interact in ways that amplify the overall risk. Among natural causes, the most significant is heavy and sustained rainfall, particularly during the monsoon season from June to September. The Western Ghats — a mountain range running along India's southwestern coast — receives some of the highest annual rainfall totals in the world, and the soil in these hills becomes saturated so rapidly during intense downpours that even relatively gentle slopes can fail within hours. Seismic activity is another natural trigger: the Himalayan region is one of the most geologically active zones on the planet, and earthquakes can loosen rock formations that were previously stable, leaving them vulnerable to subsequent rainfall or even moderate vibration.", "Human activities have significantly compounded the natural landslide risk in many parts of India. The construction of roads and highways through mountain terrain — a process that typically involves cutting into hillsides and removing the vegetation that anchors soil in place — is widely regarded as one of the most damaging interventions. In the state of Uttarakhand alone, thousands of kilometers of new roads have been built through ecologically fragile mountain areas over the past three decades, frequently without adequate geological assessment or slope stabilization measures. Deforestation for agricultural expansion and timber harvesting similarly removes root systems that would otherwise hold soil together, accelerating erosion and increasing the likelihood of slope failure during heavy rain.", "Rapid and often unplanned urbanization in landslide-prone areas has created additional layers of risk. In many hill towns of Himachal Pradesh, Sikkim, and Meghalaya, residential and commercial construction has taken place on slopes that were previously considered too steep or unstable for development. The additional weight of buildings, the disruption caused by excavation, and the increased surface runoff generated by roads and paved areas all contribute to greater slope instability over time. When a major landslide does occur in such settings, the consequences are far more severe than they would have been in an undeveloped landscape, because densely built structures concentrate both human population and economic assets in the path of the moving debris.", "The consequences of major landslides in India extend well beyond the immediate loss of life and property. Transportation networks — many of which pass through narrow mountain corridors with no alternative routing — can be blocked for days or weeks, cutting off communities from food, medical supplies, and emergency services. In the aftermath of the 2013 Uttarakhand floods and associated landslides, which killed more than five thousand people and displaced tens of thousands more, entire villages were inaccessible for extended periods due to the destruction of road and bridge infrastructure. Agricultural land buried under debris may take years or even decades to recover, and the psychological impact on surviving communities, including post-traumatic stress and prolonged economic hardship, is rarely quantified in official damage assessments.", "Early warning systems for landslides in India remain underdeveloped compared to those for other natural hazards such as cyclones and floods. The Geological Survey of India and several state disaster management authorities have begun mapping high-risk zones and installing monitoring equipment in selected areas, but the scale of the country's landslide-prone terrain — estimated at more than 12% of its total land area — makes comprehensive surveillance extremely challenging. Community-based early warning approaches, in which local residents are trained to recognize signs of imminent slope instability and alert authorities, have shown promise in pilot projects in Kerala and Darjeeling, but have not yet been scaled up nationwide.", "Reducing landslide risk in India over the long term will require coordinated action across multiple policy domains, from land use regulation and infrastructure planning to forest conservation and climate adaptation. The frequency and intensity of extreme rainfall events in many parts of India is projected to increase as a consequence of climate change, which will likely raise the baseline landslide risk even if all other contributing factors remain constant. Building resilience into both physical infrastructure and community preparedness frameworks is therefore not merely a technical challenge but a governance and social equity issue — one that disproportionately affects the rural poor who often have the fewest resources to anticipate, withstand, or recover from such disasters."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of the passage?", options: ["To argue that the Indian government has completely failed to protect its citizens from landslide disasters.", "To examine the natural and human causes of landslides in India, their consequences, and the challenges involved in managing them.", "To describe the geological history of the Himalayas and explain how mountain ranges form over time.", "To compare the frequency of landslides in India with that of other natural disasters such as floods and cyclones."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is the monsoon season particularly associated with landslides in the Western Ghats?", options: ["Because strong monsoon winds loosen rock formations along the ridges of the Western Ghats.", "Because monsoon flooding causes rivers to overflow and erode the base of hillsides throughout the region.", "Because the Western Ghats lack the dense vegetation needed to absorb rainfall during the monsoon months.", "Because the region receives very high annual rainfall, causing soil to become saturated quickly even on gentle slopes."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is road construction through mountain terrain considered particularly damaging in terms of landslide risk?", options: ["Because roads redirect rainfall into rivers, causing sudden floods that erode the base of slopes.", "Because roads bring large numbers of tourists whose activities disturb fragile mountain ecosystems.", "Because the vibration caused by heavy vehicles on mountain roads triggers rockfalls and slope failures.", "Because cutting into hillsides and removing vegetation destroys the natural systems that hold soil in place."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following human activities is NOT mentioned as a factor that increases landslide risk in India?", options: ["The use of chemical fertilizers on hillside farmland that weakens soil structure.", "Residential and commercial construction on steep or unstable slopes.", "Deforestation for agricultural expansion and timber harvesting.", "Road and highway construction through ecologically fragile mountain terrain."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following consequences resulted from the 2013 Uttarakhand disaster?", options: ["Agricultural land in the affected area was fully restored within two years through government-funded programs.", "Entire villages were cut off from essential services for extended periods due to destroyed road and bridge infrastructure.", "The Indian government immediately launched a nationwide early warning system for landslides.", "The disaster caused fewer than one thousand deaths but displaced more than a million people."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has shown promise as an approach to landslide early warning in India?", options: ["Requiring all construction projects in high-risk zones to include automated landslide alert systems.", "Developing a national network of underground sensors that can detect slope movement in real time.", "Training community members to identify signs of slope instability and report them to authorities.", "The installation of satellite monitoring systems across all landslide-prone regions of the country."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"destabilizes\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["makes unsteady or weakens the structure of", "gradually wears away through water flow", "removes completely from a slope", "strengthens and compacts"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"compounded\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["accurately measured and recorded", "reduced and brought under control", "made worse by the addition of further factors", "transferred from one region to another"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"those\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["the early warning systems developed for other natural hazards such as cyclones and floods.", "the landslide monitoring systems currently being installed in selected areas.", "the high-risk zones mapped by the Geological Survey of India.", "the community-based pilot projects carried out in Kerala and Darjeeling."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Based on paragraph 7, what can be inferred about the author's view of landslide risk management in India?", options: ["The author considers landslide risk reduction to be both a technical challenge and a matter of social equity, with the rural poor bearing a disproportionate burden.", "The author argues that responsibility for landslide prevention should rest entirely with local communities rather than with the national government.", "The author is optimistic that climate change will have little additional effect on landslide frequency in India.", "The author believes that technical engineering solutions alone are sufficient to solve the landslide problem."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Teleconsultation for Women's Healthcare in Singapore", paragraphs: ["Singapore's healthcare system has long been regarded as one of the most efficient and technologically advanced in Asia, yet even within this high-performing context, certain groups of patients have historically encountered persistent barriers to accessing timely and appropriate medical care. Women, in particular, have reported difficulties in seeking consultations for sensitive reproductive and gynaecological concerns, citing factors such as time constraints, workplace inflexibility, childcare responsibilities, and a degree of social discomfort associated with discussing intimate health matters in a traditional clinic setting. The emergence of teleconsultation platforms over the past decade has begun to address some of these structural obstacles, offering new pathways for women to engage with healthcare professionals from private and convenient locations.", "Teleconsultation refers to the use of digital communication technologies — most commonly video calls, secure messaging applications, and dedicated healthcare apps — to conduct medical consultations remotely, without requiring the patient to be physically present at a clinic or hospital. In Singapore, the regulatory framework governing teleconsultation has evolved considerably since its initial introduction. The Singapore Medical Council updated its ethical guidelines in 2018 to formally permit telemedicine for a wider range of conditions, and subsequent policy developments, accelerated significantly by the COVID-19 pandemic, further normalized its use across the healthcare system. By 2022, multiple government-linked and private teleconsultation providers had established dedicated services for women's health, covering areas such as contraception advice, menstrual irregularities, urinary tract infections, perimenopause management, and mental health support.", "The perceived benefits of teleconsultation for women's healthcare are well documented in patient satisfaction surveys and clinical outcome studies conducted across the region. Chief among them is the elimination of travel time and waiting room delays, which is particularly significant for working mothers and women in caregiving roles who find it difficult to take extended time away from professional or domestic responsibilities. Several studies conducted in Singapore's restructured hospital network have found that teleconsultation appointment adherence rates are notably higher than those for equivalent in-person appointments, suggesting that the convenience of remote access translates directly into more consistent engagement with healthcare services. Patients also consistently report greater comfort in discussing sensitive topics — including sexual health, fertility concerns, and mental health symptoms — via teleconsultation than in face-to-face settings, a finding that has been attributed to the psychological distance created by the digital interface.", "Despite these advantages, teleconsultation is not without its clinical and ethical limitations in the context of women's healthcare. A number of gynaecological conditions — including suspected cervical abnormalities, pelvic inflammatory disease, and complications arising from pregnancy — require physical examination, diagnostic imaging, or laboratory testing that cannot be replicated remotely. The risk of misdiagnosis or delayed diagnosis in such cases is a genuine concern among medical practitioners, and several professional bodies in Singapore have issued guidance emphasizing that teleconsultation should function as a complement to, rather than a replacement for, in-person care. Data privacy presents an additional layer of concern: the sensitive nature of reproductive and mental health information makes the security of teleconsultation platforms a matter of considerable importance, and not all commercially available applications meet the standards required by Singapore's Personal Data Protection Act.", "[1] Access and equity considerations complicate the picture further. While teleconsultation is generally well suited to Singapore's densely connected digital infrastructure, its benefits are not uniformly distributed across the population. Older women, women with lower levels of digital literacy, and those from lower-income households may face significant barriers to using teleconsultation platforms effectively, including limited access to appropriate devices, unfamiliarity with app-based interfaces, and reduced capacity to navigate the administrative processes involved in booking and attending virtual appointments. [2] Without deliberate policy interventions to bridge these gaps, there is a risk that teleconsultation could inadvertently widen existing health inequities rather than reduce them. [3] Some public health researchers have argued that addressing digital exclusion in healthcare requires not only infrastructure investment but also targeted health literacy programs that help disadvantaged groups develop the skills and confidence needed to use digital health services effectively. [4]", "The integration of teleconsultation into Singapore's broader women's healthcare landscape is still at a relatively early stage, and important questions about optimal service design, clinical governance, and long-term health outcomes remain open. Nevertheless, the trajectory of adoption has been consistently upward, and there are grounds for cautious optimism. Ongoing research programs, including longitudinal studies tracking patient outcomes across both teleconsultation and in-person care pathways, are beginning to generate the evidence base needed to refine best practice guidelines. If the benefits of teleconsultation can be extended equitably and its clinical limitations managed responsibly, it holds genuine potential to reduce longstanding disparities in women's access to quality healthcare in Singapore and, more broadly, across the region."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Teleconsultation has completely resolved the barriers that women in Singapore face when seeking healthcare.", "Teleconsultation offers meaningful benefits for women's healthcare in Singapore but also presents clinical, ethical, and equity challenges that require careful management.", "The primary obstacle to women's healthcare in Singapore is a shortage of qualified gynaecological specialists rather than issues of access or convenience.", "Singapore's healthcare system is the most technologically advanced in Asia and serves as a model for the rest of the region."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which development significantly accelerated the normalization of teleconsultation in Singapore?", options: ["The COVID-19 pandemic, which prompted policy changes that further integrated teleconsultation into the healthcare system.", "The Singapore Medical Council's decision to prohibit in-person consultations for minor conditions.", "The establishment of dedicated women's health teleconsultation services by private providers in 2018.", "A government mandate requiring all public hospitals to offer teleconsultation as a primary care option."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do teleconsultation appointments have higher adherence rates than equivalent in-person appointments?", options: ["Because teleconsultation providers offer financial incentives to patients who attend their scheduled appointments.", "Because teleconsultation appointments are shorter and therefore less disruptive to a patient's daily schedule.", "Because the convenience of remote access makes it easier for women to keep their appointments without disrupting other responsibilities.", "Because digital platforms send automatic reminders that prevent patients from forgetting their appointments."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"address\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["formally describe in writing", "publicly announce", "deal with or begin to tackle", "locate and identify"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following conditions is cited as an example that requires in-person care and cannot be managed through teleconsultation alone?", options: ["Suspected cervical abnormalities that require physical examination or diagnostic imaging.", "Mental health conditions that require ongoing pharmacological treatment.", "Urinary tract infections that recur more than twice in a single year.", "Menstrual irregularities that have not responded to initial hormonal treatment."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a barrier that disadvantaged women may face when using teleconsultation?", options: ["Unfamiliarity with app-based interfaces.", "Limited access to suitable digital devices.", "Difficulty navigating the booking and appointment processes.", "Language barriers that prevent effective communication with healthcare providers."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"attributed\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["disputed by researchers in subsequent studies", "proven beyond reasonable doubt", "officially recorded in clinical documentation", "credited to or explained as being caused by"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the working mothers and women in caregiving roles mentioned earlier in the paragraph.", "the perceived benefits of teleconsultation for women's healthcare.", "the patient satisfaction surveys and clinical outcome studies cited in the same sentence.", "the restructured hospitals in Singapore where the relevant studies were conducted."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 4 and 5, what can be inferred about the most effective approach to deploying teleconsultation in women's healthcare?", options: ["Teleconsultation should entirely replace in-person care to maximize efficiency and reduce costs across the healthcare system.", "Teleconsultation is most effective as a standalone service when delivered through government-regulated platforms only.", "The clinical limitations of teleconsultation are so significant that its use should be restricted to mental health and contraception services only.", "Teleconsultation works best when integrated alongside in-person care and supported by policies that ensure equitable access across all demographic groups."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to paragraph 5? \"This disparity is especially pronounced among elderly women and recent migrants who may have had limited prior exposure to digital health platforms.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Evolutionary and Nutritional Science of Breastfeeding", paragraphs: ["Few physiological processes in human biology carry as much evolutionary, immunological, and social significance as lactation — the production of breast milk following childbirth. In an era of increasingly sophisticated infant formula products, the scientific study of human breast milk has undergone a remarkable renaissance , driven by the recognition that its composition is far more complex than any manufactured substitute has yet succeeded in replicating. What emerges from contemporary research is not a simple portrait of milk as nutritional fuel but a dynamic, biologically active substance whose properties shift in response to the needs of the individual infant, the health status of the mother, and even the time of day. [1] Understanding these dimensions of breast milk, scientists argue, is essential not only for optimizing infant health outcomes but for illuminating fundamental questions about the co-evolution of human mothers and their offspring. [2]", "From an evolutionary standpoint, human lactation is distinguished from that of most other mammals by its extraordinary duration and compositional flexibility. The extended period of breastfeeding observed in traditional hunter-gatherer societies — typically two to four years per child — reflects a reproductive strategy in which prolonged maternal investment in infant survival is traded against the spacing of subsequent pregnancies. Breast milk's caloric composition shifts considerably over this period: colostrum, the thick, protein-rich fluid produced in the first days after birth, is dominated by immunoglobulins and growth factors rather than fat and lactose, which become more prominent as the infant matures. This calibration is not coincidental — colostrum functions less as a food source and more as an initial immune primer, seeding the newborn gut with maternal antibodies at a developmental stage when the infant's own immune system is not yet capable of mounting an effective defense.", "The immunological dimension of human breast milk has attracted particular scientific attention in recent decades. Milk contains not only passive immune factors such as secretory immunoglobulin A, lactoferrin, and lysozyme, but also living immune cells — predominantly macrophages, neutrophils, and lymphocytes — that are transferred from mother to infant with each feeding. Research conducted over the past two decades has revealed that these transferred cells are not merely inert passengers; some appear to survive passage through the infant's gastrointestinal tract and may retain immunological function in the recipient's body. The full implications of this cellular transfer are not yet understood, but several research groups have proposed that it may contribute to the training of the infant immune system in ways that persist well beyond the period of breastfeeding itself.", "Perhaps the most striking recent discovery in breast milk research concerns the role of human milk oligosaccharides — a class of complex carbohydrate molecules that are the third most abundant component of breast milk after fat and lactose, yet cannot be digested by the infant at all. For decades, their presence in such quantities was regarded as a puzzling anomaly. The resolution came with the recognition that human milk oligosaccharides serve not as nutrition for the infant but as a highly selective prebiotic substrate for specific strains of gut bacteria — most notably Bifidobacterium longum subsp. infantis — that are critical to the development of a healthy intestinal microbiome in early life. The composition of the oligosaccharide fraction is not uniform across mothers; it varies according to genetic blood type, geography, and individual biological variation, raising the possibility that breast milk is, in part, customized to establish a gut microbial community suited to the infant's specific immunological and metabolic environment.", "The relationship between breastfeeding duration and long-term health outcomes has been extensively studied in epidemiological research, though establishing causal relationships in this domain is methodologically challenging. Observational data consistently associate longer breastfeeding with reduced rates of respiratory infections, gastrointestinal illness, allergic disease, and childhood obesity in the infant, as well as reduced maternal risk of breast and ovarian cancer. Critics of this literature point out that breastfeeding behavior is strongly confounded by socioeconomic status, maternal education, and access to healthcare — factors that are themselves independently associated with better health outcomes — making it difficult to isolate the biological effect of breast milk from the broader context of advantaged caregiving. Sophisticated statistical approaches, including sibling-comparison studies designed to control for shared family environment, have attempted to disentangle these effects, with results that generally support a modest but genuine independent benefit of breastfeeding.", "The nutritional science of breast milk has direct implications for the development of infant formula, which remains an essential resource for mothers who are unable or choose not to breastfeed. Despite significant advances, current formula products cannot replicate the immunological activity, cellular content, or oligosaccharide diversity of human milk. Some manufacturers have begun incorporating synthetic oligosaccharides into premium formula products, and there is ongoing research into the possibility of adding lactoferrin and other bioactive proteins. Whether these additions can meaningfully close the gap between formula and breast milk — or whether the adaptive, dynamic nature of breast milk composition makes full replication fundamentally impossible — remains an open question that continues to drive both commercial investment and basic scientific inquiry.", "What the emerging science of breast milk ultimately reveals is that lactation is not a passive nutritional delivery mechanism but an active biological dialogue between mother and infant — one shaped by millions of years of co-evolutionary pressure and still only partially decoded by modern science. The recognition that breast milk is adaptive, immunologically active, and ecologically calibrated to the individual infant challenges reductive accounts of infant nutrition and invites a more nuanced appreciation of the biological complexity embedded in what is, for many families around the world, simply the most natural act of early parenthood."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best states the central argument of the passage?", options: ["The scientific study of breast milk reveals it to be a biologically active, adaptive substance of extraordinary complexity whose full properties modern science has yet to fully decode.", "The primary value of breastfeeding lies in its epidemiological association with reduced rates of childhood disease rather than in any specific biochemical property.", "Advances in formula manufacturing have nearly closed the nutritional gap between breast milk and commercially produced substitutes.", "Human breast milk is superior to infant formula in every measurable respect, and formula should be used only as a last resort."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does colostrum differ functionally from the breast milk produced at later stages of infant development?", options: ["Colostrum is nutritionally equivalent to mature breast milk but is produced only during the first twenty-four hours after birth.", "Colostrum contains higher levels of fat and lactose than mature milk, making it a more efficient energy source for newborns.", "Colostrum functions primarily as an immune primer by delivering maternal antibodies to the newborn gut rather than serving as a primary caloric food source.", "Colostrum is produced in greater volumes than mature milk to compensate for the newborn's smaller stomach capacity."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"renaissance\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a collaborative effort between commercial and academic researchers", "an initial phase of discovery following years of complete neglect", "a period of standardization and regulatory oversight", "a significant revival of interest and development"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the primary function of human milk oligosaccharides?", options: ["To regulate the rate at which the infant absorbs fat and lactose from breast milk during each feeding.", "To neutralize harmful pathogens in the infant's gastrointestinal tract before they can cause infection.", "To act as a selective prebiotic that nourishes specific beneficial gut bacteria critical to the infant's microbiome development.", "To provide a concentrated source of easily digestible carbohydrates that support rapid infant brain development."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is it methodologically difficult to establish that breastfeeding itself causes better long-term health outcomes?", options: ["Because most studies on breastfeeding have been conducted in high-income countries and cannot be generalized to other populations.", "Because the immunological components of breast milk vary so widely between individual mothers that no general conclusions can be drawn.", "Because breastfeeding behavior is strongly associated with socioeconomic and educational factors that independently predict better health outcomes, making it hard to isolate breastfeeding's biological effect.", "Because the health benefits of breastfeeding only become statistically apparent after several decades, making longitudinal tracking impractical."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following health outcomes is NOT mentioned as being associated with longer breastfeeding duration?", options: ["Lower risk of breast and ovarian cancer in the mother.", "Decreased incidence of childhood obesity in the infant.", "Improved cognitive development and higher academic achievement in the child.", "Reduced rates of allergic disease in the infant."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"confounded\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["disproven by subsequent experimental evidence", "strengthened by the presence of multiple supporting variables", "confirmed through rigorous statistical reanalysis", "distorted by the influence of additional variables that obscure a causal relationship"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the living immune cells found in human breast milk.", "the infant's gastrointestinal tract through which transferred cells must pass.", "the cellular transfer of immune cells from mother to infant via breast milk.", "the immunological function that transferred cells appear to retain after passage."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 4 and 6, what does the author imply about the feasibility of fully replicating breast milk through manufactured formula?", options: ["The main barrier to replication is regulatory rather than scientific, as existing technology could already produce an equivalent product.", "The adaptive and dynamic nature of breast milk composition may make complete replication fundamentally impossible, though incremental improvements in formula remain valuable.", "Replication is unnecessary because the immunological benefits of breast milk are negligible once an infant reaches six months of age.", "Full replication is achievable within the next decade given current rates of progress in nutritional science."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to paragraph 1? \"This bidirectional exchange suggests that the act of feeding is simultaneously an act of immune communication.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "Both [1] and [2]", "None of the above"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-073", num: 73, title: "Đề 073", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Rich History of Coffee", paragraphs: ["Coffee is one of the most popular drinks in the world today. Millions of people enjoy a cup of coffee every morning to help them wake up and feel alert. But coffee is much more than just a morning routine. It has a long and fascinating history that stretches back hundreds of years, crossing continents and changing cultures along the way. Understanding where coffee comes from can help us appreciate this beloved drink in a whole new way.", "The story of coffee is believed to have begun in the highlands of Ethiopia, a country in East Africa. According to a popular legend, a young goat herder named Kaldi first noticed the effects of coffee around the ninth century. He observed that his goats became unusually energetic after eating the red berries from a certain type of tree. Curious about what he had seen, Kaldi brought some of the berries to a local monastery. The monks decided to make a drink from the berries and discovered that it helped them stay awake during long hours of evening prayer. Word of this discovery gradually spread across the region.", "From Ethiopia, coffee made its way to the Arabian Peninsula, particularly to the region now known as Yemen, sometime during the fifteenth century. It was in Yemen that people first began to cultivate coffee plants in organized farms. Coffee quickly became an important part of Arabian culture and society. Coffeehouses, known as \"qahveh khaneh,\" began to appear in cities across the Middle East. These establishments were popular meeting places where people gathered to drink coffee, listen to music, play chess, and discuss the events of the day. They were sometimes called \"Schools of the Wise\" because of the lively conversations and debates that took place inside them.", "By the seventeenth century, coffee had arrived in Europe, brought by traders and travelers from the East. At first, some people were suspicious of this new dark drink and its unfamiliar effects. In certain cities, religious leaders raised concerns about whether coffee was an appropriate beverage. However, these concerns were eventually overcome, and coffee became widely accepted across the continent. Coffeehouses opened throughout England, France, and other European countries. Like those in the Middle East, European coffeehouses became important social spaces where businesspeople, artists, politicians, and ordinary citizens could meet and exchange ideas.", "Coffee also played a significant role in the history of the Americas. European colonists brought coffee plants to the Caribbean and South America in the early eighteenth century. The climate and soil conditions in countries such as Brazil, Colombia, and Guatemala proved to be ideal for growing coffee. Over time, these regions became some of the largest coffee producers in the world. Today, Brazil alone produces nearly one-third of the world's total coffee supply, making it the top coffee-producing nation on the planet.", "The way people prepare and enjoy coffee has also changed greatly over time. For centuries, coffee was simply boiled with water and served as a strong, sometimes bitter drink. The invention of new brewing methods, such as the espresso machine in Italy in the early twentieth century, introduced faster and more varied ways of making coffee. Today, coffee lovers can choose from a wide range of options, including cappuccinos, lattes, cold brews, and many other specialty drinks. Coffee shops around the world have become creative spaces that offer not just beverages, but entire experiences designed around this remarkable drink.", "Despite all the changes in how coffee is made and consumed, its central role in human social life has remained remarkably consistent . From the ancient monasteries of Ethiopia to the busy coffee shops of modern cities, coffee has always been a drink that brings people together. Whether shared between friends, enjoyed alone in a quiet moment, or sipped during an important business meeting, coffee continues to be one of the most meaningful and widely shared daily rituals across cultures and generations."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The most important aspect of coffee's history is its arrival in South America.", "Coffee became popular primarily because of the invention of new brewing machines.", "Coffee was invented in Yemen and later spread to Africa and Europe.", "Coffee has a long history that has shaped cultures and brought people together across the world."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the red berries from the coffee tree", "the goats that Kaldi was herding", "the people living near Kaldi's village", "the monks at the local monastery"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why were coffeehouses in the Middle East sometimes called \"Schools of the Wise\"?", options: ["They offered formal lessons in music, chess, and foreign languages.", "They were known for the lively conversations and intellectual debates that took place inside them.", "They were managed by religious leaders who shared their knowledge with customers.", "They were built next to universities where scholars and teachers worked."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how did some Europeans initially react to the arrival of coffee?", options: ["They were suspicious of it and some religious leaders raised concerns about it.", "They imported it in large quantities and quickly made it the most popular drink in Europe.", "They tried to ban it from coffeehouses to protect traditional social customs.", "They welcomed it immediately as a healthy substitute for alcohol."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is Brazil's position in global coffee production today?", options: ["It produces about half of the world's total coffee supply.", "It is the second-largest coffee producer after Colombia.", "It recently surpassed Guatemala to become the leading coffee-growing nation.", "It produces nearly one-third of the world's total coffee supply, making it the top producer."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"cultivate\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["grow and farm", "protect", "discover", "sell"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which invention introduced faster and more varied ways of making coffee?", options: ["The espresso machine invented in Italy in the early twentieth century", "The cold brew filtration system developed in France", "The steam-powered coffee press first used in England", "The automatic drip coffee maker created in Germany"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an activity that took place in Middle Eastern coffeehouses?", options: ["Reading books and newspapers", "Playing chess", "Listening to music", "Discussing the events of the day"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"consistent\" in the final paragraph is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["gradually becoming weaker", "surprisingly unpredictable", "largely unchanged and steady", "increasingly uncertain"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the author's attitude toward coffee?", options: ["The author thinks coffee's popularity is mostly the result of clever marketing by producers.", "The author is mainly interested in the commercial aspects of the coffee industry.", "The author believes coffee is overrated compared to other historical drinks.", "The author views coffee with admiration as a drink of great historical and cultural significance."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Evolving Face of Modern Tourism", paragraphs: ["Tourism has long been one of the world's largest and most dynamic industries. Every year, billions of people travel across borders and time zones in search of new experiences, cultural encounters, and personal rejuvenation. For decades, the dominant model of tourism was relatively straightforward: tourists flew to a well-known destination, stayed in a large hotel, visited famous landmarks, and returned home with photographs and souvenirs. However, this familiar pattern has been shifting dramatically in recent years, driven by changes in technology, traveler values, and global awareness of environmental and social issues.", "One of the most significant developments in modern tourism is the rise of experience-based travel. Rather than simply visiting a place, today's travelers increasingly seek to immerse themselves in local culture, learn new skills, or engage in meaningful activities. Cooking classes in rural Tuscany, traditional weaving workshops in Peru, and wildlife conservation projects in Kenya are just a few examples of the kinds of experiences that attract modern tourists. This shift reflects a broader cultural change in which people value personal growth and authentic connection over passive sightseeing. Travel companies have responded to this trend by designing programs that place cultural participation, rather than mere observation, at the center of the tourist experience.", "Technology has played a transformative role in reshaping how people plan, book, and share their travel experiences. The rise of online booking platforms, travel blogs, and social media has democratized access to travel information, allowing independent travelers to research and arrange complex trips without relying on traditional travel agencies. At the same time, platforms such as short-term rental services have disrupted the hotel industry by offering travelers the option of staying in local homes rather than standardized hotel rooms. Critics of these platforms argue that they have contributed to rising housing costs in popular tourist destinations by converting long-term rental properties into short-term holiday accommodation. Supporters, however, contend that such services allow travelers to experience destinations in a more authentic and community-embedded way.", "Sustainable tourism — sometimes called eco-tourism or responsible travel — has emerged as one of the most discussed and debated trends in the industry. As awareness of climate change and environmental degradation has grown, many travelers have begun to question the ecological footprint of their journeys. Long-haul flights, in particular, have come under scrutiny for their significant contribution to greenhouse gas emissions. In response, a growing number of travelers are choosing to offset their carbon footprints, select environmentally certified accommodations, or opt for slower forms of travel such as rail journeys that allow for greater appreciation of the landscape while producing fewer emissions. Some environmental advocates, however, argue that these measures are insufficient and that the only genuinely sustainable choice is to travel less frequently overall.", "The COVID-19 pandemic represented an unprecedented disruption to global tourism, temporarily halting international travel on a scale never before seen in the modern era. At its peak, the pandemic forced the closure of borders, grounded fleets of aircraft, and left hotels, restaurants, and tourist attractions around the world empty and financially devastated. Yet the crisis also prompted a profound period of reflection within the industry. Many destinations that had previously struggled with the negative effects of overtourism — including damage to heritage sites, strain on local infrastructure, and tension between residents and visitors — found an unexpected relief in the sudden absence of mass tourism. This pause encouraged some governments and communities to reconsider their approach, exploring models of tourism that prioritize quality of experience and environmental respect over sheer visitor numbers.", "One concept that gained renewed attention during and after the pandemic is the idea of \"slow travel.\" In contrast to the traditional holiday model of visiting as many destinations as possible within a short time, slow travel encourages visitors to spend extended periods in a single location, forging deeper connections with local communities and developing a more nuanced understanding of the places they visit. Proponents of slow travel argue that it benefits not only the traveler — through reduced stress and richer experiences — but also the destination, by distributing tourist spending more evenly across local businesses rather than concentrating it in a few highly commercialized attractions.", "As tourism continues to evolve, the industry faces the challenge of balancing economic necessity with cultural preservation and environmental responsibility. The communities that depend on tourism for their livelihoods have a legitimate interest in welcoming visitors, yet the unchecked growth of mass tourism can erode the very qualities that make a destination worth visiting in the first place. Finding this balance will require ongoing collaboration between governments, businesses, local communities, and travelers themselves. The future of tourism, most analysts agree, will be defined not merely by where people go, but by how thoughtfully and responsibly they choose to go there."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Modern tourism is evolving significantly due to changes in traveler values, technology, and environmental awareness.", "The COVID-19 pandemic permanently destroyed the global tourism industry.", "Technology has made it easier for large hotels to attract more international tourists.", "Slow travel is the best solution to the environmental problems caused by mass tourism."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"democratized\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["reduced in quality due to excessive competition", "transferred from private companies to public ownership", "regulated more strictly by government authorities", "made available and accessible to a much wider range of people"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes the type of travel experiences sought by modern tourists?", options: ["Visiting as many famous landmarks as possible within a short period of time.", "Traveling to remote locations where no tourist infrastructure has been developed.", "Participating in local cultural activities and engaging with communities in a meaningful way.", "Staying in luxury hotels and enjoying high-end dining and entertainment."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what criticism has been directed at short-term rental platforms?", options: ["They collect too much personal data from travelers who use their services.", "They have made it more difficult for independent travelers to plan complex trips.", "They have caused housing costs in popular tourist areas to rise by converting long-term rentals into holiday accommodation.", "They offer lower quality accommodation than traditional hotels."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why have long-haul flights come under particular scrutiny from environmental advocates?", options: ["They make a significant contribution to greenhouse gas emissions.", "They prevent travelers from experiencing the landscapes they pass through.", "They are significantly more expensive than other forms of travel.", "They frequently cause noise pollution in communities near major airports."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["independent travelers who plan their own trips", "traditional travel agencies", "critics of the modern tourism industry", "short-term rental platforms"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does slow travel benefit local destinations?", options: ["It prevents environmental damage by limiting the use of motor vehicles near tourist areas.", "It allows local governments to charge higher prices for entry to heritage sites.", "It reduces the total number of tourists visiting a destination each year.", "It encourages travelers to spend money more evenly across local businesses rather than only at major attractions."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit of slow travel?", options: ["A more even distribution of tourist spending among local businesses", "Reduced stress for the traveler", "A significant decrease in carbon emissions from international flights", "Deeper connections with local communities"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"erode\" in the final paragraph is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["gradually wear away or destroy", "strengthen and improve over time", "quickly and completely replace", "carefully preserve and protect"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about the author's view of the future of tourism?", options: ["The author implies that the quality and responsibility of travel matter as much as the destinations people choose.", "The author believes that mass tourism will inevitably decline as travelers become more environmentally conscious.", "The author argues that slow travel will completely replace traditional tourism within the next decade.", "The author suggests that the tourism industry should be taken over by governments to ensure responsible management."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Rise of E-Sports as a Global Phenomenon", paragraphs: ["Not long ago, the idea of professional video gaming would have seemed absurd to most people. Playing games was widely regarded as a leisure activity for teenagers, something to be done in spare time and certainly not a career path worthy of serious consideration. Yet within the span of roughly two decades, competitive gaming — now widely known as e-sports — has transformed into a multi-billion-dollar global industry that commands sold-out arenas, dedicated television channels, and prize pools that rival those of traditional athletic competitions. The speed and scale of this transformation have surprised even the most optimistic figures within the gaming community.", "The roots of competitive gaming stretch back to the early days of arcade culture in the 1970s and 1980s, when players competed for high scores on machines in local venues. [1] The emergence of the internet in the 1990s proved to be the true catalyst for e-sports as we recognize it today. Online connectivity allowed players from different cities and countries to compete against one another in real time, removing the geographical limitations that had previously confined competition to local communities. Games such as StarCraft in South Korea became cultural touchstones during this era, drawing large television audiences and producing players who were celebrated as national celebrities. [2] South Korea's early investment in broadband infrastructure gave it a decisive head start in cultivating a professional gaming ecosystem that other countries would later seek to replicate .", "The explosive growth of e-sports in the twenty-first century has been driven by several converging forces. Streaming platforms such as Twitch and YouTube Gaming have allowed audiences to watch live competitive play at any hour, from any location, creating a global viewership that traditional sports broadcasters have struggled to match among younger demographics. Major tournaments for games like League of Legends, Dota 2, and Counter-Strike have regularly attracted tens of millions of concurrent online viewers — figures comparable to, and in some cases exceeding, the audiences of major traditional sporting events. [3] Simultaneously, investment from mainstream corporate sponsors, sports franchise owners, and even national Olympic committees has lent the industry a degree of institutional legitimacy it previously lacked.", "Despite its remarkable commercial achievements, e-sports continues to face a number of significant challenges. One of the most persistent concerns relates to the physical and psychological wellbeing of professional players. Unlike traditional athletes, whose careers are typically extended through physical conditioning programs developed over many decades, e-sports organizations are only beginning to grapple with the occupational health implications of their profession. Repetitive strain injuries affecting the hands, wrists, and forearms are common among players who train for eight to twelve hours daily. Mental health pressures, including performance anxiety, burnout, and the psychological difficulty of retiring from competition at a relatively young age — most professionals peak in their mid-twenties — represent equally serious concerns that the industry is only starting to address systematically.", "The question of longevity also applies to the games themselves. Unlike traditional sports, where the rules of football or basketball remain essentially stable across generations, competitive e-sports titles are subject to constant updates, patches, and balance changes issued by their developers. A game that dominates the competitive scene today may become obsolete or lose its player base within a few years as newer titles emerge. This structural instability poses a unique challenge for players, organizations, and sponsors who invest heavily in a particular game's ecosystem, only to see audience interest shift to a new title. [4] Some analysts have compared this dynamic to building a professional sports league on a foundation that the governing body can redraw at will.", "The relationship between e-sports and traditional athletics has evolved from one of mutual dismissal to cautious engagement. Several established sports organizations, including major football clubs in Europe and basketball franchises in North America, have acquired e-sports teams as extensions of their existing brands. The International Olympic Committee has explored pathways for including e-sports in future Games, though debates continue over which titles qualify under the Committee's guidelines regarding the promotion of physical activity and non-violent content. These conversations reflect a broader cultural negotiation over what constitutes legitimate athletic competition in an era when skill, strategy, and reaction time — rather than physical strength alone — increasingly define high-level performance.", "Looking ahead, the trajectory of e-sports appears to depend on the industry's ability to resolve tensions between rapid commercial growth and the sustainable development of its human talent. Building robust support systems for players — including career transition programs, mental health resources, and standardized contracts — will be essential if e-sports organizations hope to retain the trust of both players and audiences over the long term. What is already beyond dispute is that competitive gaming has permanently altered the global entertainment landscape, establishing itself not as a passing trend but as a defining cultural and economic force of the twenty-first century."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central concern of the passage?", options: ["The passage argues that e-sports will eventually overtake traditional athletics in global popularity.", "The passage criticizes the lack of government support for professional e-sports players in most countries.", "The passage examines the rapid growth of e-sports as a global industry while also exploring the significant challenges it faces.", "The passage focuses primarily on the history of competitive gaming from its origins in arcade culture."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what advantage did South Korea gain in the early development of e-sports?", options: ["Its universities offered the earliest academic programs in game design and e-sports management.", "It was the first country to establish a national e-sports regulatory body.", "It produced the world's first streaming platform dedicated exclusively to competitive gaming.", "Its early investment in broadband infrastructure gave it a head start in building a professional gaming ecosystem."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Based on information from both paragraphs 3 and 6, which combination of factors has most contributed to giving e-sports greater institutional legitimacy?", options: ["The development of faster internet connections and the construction of dedicated e-sports arenas in major cities.", "The prize money offered at major tournaments and the endorsement deals signed by individual star players.", "The introduction of standardized contracts for players and the creation of national e-sports governing bodies.", "The global viewership generated by streaming platforms and the investment and engagement of mainstream sports organizations and Olympic committees."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a health challenge faced by professional e-sports players?", options: ["Psychological difficulty associated with early retirement from competition", "Repetitive strain injuries to the hands and wrists", "Performance anxiety and burnout", "Visual impairment caused by prolonged screen exposure"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does the constant updating of competitive game titles pose a unique challenge for the e-sports industry?", options: ["Game developers use updates to restrict access to competitive features, forcing players to pay additional fees.", "Frequent changes to game rules make it difficult for fans to follow competitive matches without prior experience.", "Players, organizations, and sponsors who invest in a particular game risk losing their investment if audience interest shifts to newer titles.", "Updates frequently introduce technical errors that make fair competition impossible."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has prevented straightforward inclusion of e-sports in the Olympic Games?", options: ["Disagreements among game developers over which titles should be selected for official competition", "The high cost of broadcasting e-sports events to a global television audience", "A lack of interest from national Olympic committees in most countries", "Ongoing debates over whether e-sports titles meet guidelines on physical activity and non-violent content"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What conclusion can be drawn by combining information from paragraphs 4 and 7 about the long-term sustainability of e-sports as an industry?", options: ["E-sports will only achieve long-term sustainability once it is formally recognized as an Olympic sport.", "The industry's future depends on its ability to address player wellbeing through structured support systems alongside its commercial growth.", "The primary obstacle to e-sports sustainability is the lack of investment from mainstream sports franchise owners.", "E-sports will remain unsustainable until game developers agree to stabilize their titles and cease issuing major updates."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"its\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["the streaming platform industry", "e-sports as a global industry", "the professional gaming community in South Korea", "the mainstream corporate sponsorship sector"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"replicate\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["gradually replace", "critically evaluate", "copy and reproduce", "compete against"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to the passage? \"These early competitions, however, were limited in reach, dependent on players being physically present at the same location.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Strategic Importance of Semiconductor Chips", paragraphs: ["Few objects in the modern world are as simultaneously ubiquitous and misunderstood as the semiconductor chip. Smaller than a fingernail, yet capable of executing billions of calculations per second, these microscopic devices underpin virtually every aspect of contemporary technological infrastructure — from the smartphones carried in pockets to the satellites orbiting the Earth, from the algorithms that route internet traffic to the control systems governing modern military hardware. [1] The semiconductor, in short, has become the foundational material of twenty-first century civilization, and control over its design, manufacture, and supply chain has emerged as one of the defining axes of geopolitical competition in the modern era.", "The manufacture of advanced semiconductor chips is an extraordinarily complex and capital-intensive process, concentrated in the hands of a remarkably small number of firms. At the apex of this hierarchy stands the Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company, known as TSMC, which produces the majority of the world's most advanced chips using fabrication processes measured in nanometers — units so small that individual transistors are now approaching the scale of individual atoms. The degree of technical specialization required to operate at this frontier is so profound that no country or company has yet succeeded in replicating TSMC's capabilities independently, despite years of effort and tens of billions of dollars in investment. [2] This concentration of critical production capacity in a single geographic location — Taiwan, an island whose political status remains a source of acute international tension — has introduced a systemic fragility into the global technology supply chain that was only fully appreciated following the semiconductor shortages triggered by the COVID-19 pandemic.", "The pandemic-era chip shortage offered a striking illustration of how deeply semiconductor dependency had penetrated sectors far removed from consumer electronics. Automobile manufacturers, unable to procure the relatively modest chips required for engine management systems and dashboard controls, were forced to halt production lines and slash output by millions of units. Hospital equipment suppliers struggled to fulfill orders for medical devices. Defense contractors reported delays in the delivery of advanced weapons systems. [3] These disruptions collectively revealed that the assumption of seamless, frictionless chip availability — an assumption embedded in the just-in-time supply chain models adopted by industries worldwide — was far more precarious than its architects had appreciated.", "The geopolitical dimensions of semiconductor dependency have intensified significantly in the context of strategic competition between the United States and China. Washington has pursued an aggressive policy of export controls targeting the most advanced chips and chip-making equipment, seeking to prevent Beijing from acquiring the technologies necessary to develop competitive artificial intelligence capabilities and next-generation military systems. China, for its part, has committed hundreds of billions of dollars to the development of a domestic semiconductor industry capable of achieving self-sufficiency, though independent analysts broadly assess that a gap of several years — and possibly a decade or more at the leading edge of fabrication — separates Chinese producers from their most advanced international competitors. The resulting dynamic is one in which both major powers are simultaneously dependent on a shared global supply chain and actively working to dismantle the mutual vulnerabilities that dependence creates.", "The response of Western governments to these strategic anxieties has taken the form of substantial industrial policy interventions. The United States CHIPS and Science Act, passed in 2022, committed over fifty billion dollars to subsidizing domestic semiconductor manufacturing and research, with the explicit aim of reducing reliance on Asian production and restoring a measure of fabrication capacity to American soil. The European Union launched a parallel initiative under its own Chips Act, targeting a doubling of its global market share in semiconductor production by 2030. These efforts represent a significant departure from the free-market orthodoxy that had previously dominated Western industrial policy, acknowledging implicitly that market forces alone had produced a geographically fragile and strategically vulnerable supply chain that governments now felt compelled to reshape.", "Yet the ambitions embedded in these legislative interventions confront formidable practical obstacles. Building a state-of-the-art semiconductor fabrication facility — known as a fab — requires not merely capital investment but an extraordinarily specialized workforce, a mature ecosystem of equipment suppliers and chemical producers, and a lead time measured in years rather than months. Critics of the CHIPS Act and its European counterparts have noted that the subsidies on offer, while substantial in absolute terms, may prove insufficient to overcome the accumulated advantages of established Asian producers, and that the timelines projected for meaningful domestic capacity may be significantly more optimistic than the underlying engineering and logistical realities warrant. [4]", "What the semiconductor's emergence as a strategic commodity ultimately reveals is the degree to which abstract notions of technological interdependence have acquired concrete political and security dimensions. The chip is no longer merely an engineering artifact; it has become an instrument of statecraft, a lever of economic coercion, and a symbol of the broader contest between rival visions of how the global technological order should be organized. Whether the trajectory of the coming decades leads toward a more fragmented, nationally siloed technology landscape or toward a managed form of interdependence acceptable to all major actors remains genuinely uncertain. What is not in doubt is that the decisions made in chip design laboratories, government ministries, and corporate boardrooms over the next decade will shape the contours of global power for generations to come."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the central argument of the passage?", options: ["The semiconductor chip has transcended its role as a technical device to become a critical axis of geopolitical power and strategic competition.", "The COVID-19 pandemic was the primary cause of the global semiconductor shortage and the geopolitical tensions that followed.", "Semiconductor chips should be produced by international organizations rather than by individual nations to prevent geopolitical conflict.", "Western governments have largely succeeded in reducing their dependence on Asian semiconductor producers through industrial policy."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"ubiquitous\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["extremely fragile and sensitive to environmental conditions", "recently invented and not yet widely understood", "highly classified and restricted from public knowledge", "present or found everywhere simultaneously"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following sectors was directly affected by the pandemic-era semiconductor shortage?", options: ["Automobile manufacturing and defense contracting", "Agricultural machinery and food processing", "Retail banking and financial services technology", "Commercial aviation and passenger transport"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What does the author imply about China's semiconductor self-sufficiency goals in paragraph 4?", options: ["Independent analysts believe China remains several years or more behind its most advanced international competitors despite significant investment.", "China has already achieved parity with TSMC at the leading edge of chip fabrication through sustained investment.", "China's domestic semiconductor ambitions are likely to succeed rapidly given the scale of government funding committed to the program.", "China has abandoned its semiconductor development program after concluding that closing the technology gap is not achievable."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a measure taken by Western governments in response to semiconductor supply chain vulnerabilities?", options: ["Committing funding to semiconductor research and development", "Providing subsidies for domestic semiconductor manufacturing", "Negotiating bilateral trade agreements with TSMC to guarantee chip supply during future crises", "Imposing export controls on advanced chips and chip-making equipment"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"committed\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["temporarily set aside pending the outcome of ongoing negotiations", "reluctantly agreed after considerable internal debate", "formally pledged and allocated significant financial resources", "secretly transferred through unofficial government channels"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What does the author suggest in paragraph 6 about critics of the CHIPS Act and its European equivalents?", options: ["Critics warn that the acts will accelerate the fragmentation of the global semiconductor supply chain beyond repair.", "Critics contend that the legislation unfairly disadvantages smaller technology companies that cannot access the available subsidies.", "Critics believe the acts are primarily motivated by protectionism rather than genuine security concerns.", "Critics argue that the subsidies may be insufficient and the projected timelines for building domestic capacity are likely overly optimistic."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What tension does the author identify when combining information from paragraphs 5 and 6?", options: ["The contradiction between the free-market principles that Western economies espouse and the interventionist industrial policies they have adopted to address chip supply vulnerabilities.", "The disagreement between chip designers and chip manufacturers over the pace at which new fabrication technologies should be introduced.", "The tension between American and European governments over which region should host the majority of new semiconductor fabrication facilities.", "The conflict between private semiconductor companies and governments over the appropriate level of state subsidy for domestic fabrication."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following best describes the author's attitude toward the future of the global semiconductor supply chain?", options: ["Cautiously analytical, acknowledging deep uncertainty about whether the outcome will be fragmentation or managed interdependence without advocating for either.", "Dismissive, as the author regards geopolitical concerns about semiconductors as exaggerated relative to the actual risks involved.", "Optimistic, as the author believes that Western industrial policy will successfully restore a stable and secure supply chain within a decade.", "Pessimistic, as the author concludes that conflict over semiconductor supremacy will inevitably lead to a breakdown of the global technology order."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to the passage? \"Across sectors as varied as consumer goods, logistics, and financial technology, the ripple effects of the shortage exposed the hidden depth of the global economy's reliance on a component most executives had never needed to think about.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
];
